\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Accretive logarithms and the principal branch}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{K=\ue^{\ui\frac{\pi}{3}}\bbI} act on a #{4}-dimensional
Hermitian space in \ref{fdip-009L}. Its adjoint is
#{\ue^{-\ui\frac{\pi}{3}}\bbI}, so its Hermitian part is #{\bbI /2},
which is positive definite. Along #{K(s)=\ue{\ui s}\bbI} for
#{0\le s\le\frac{\pi}{3}}, one has
#{\det K(s)=\ue^{4\ui s}}. Continuity and the normalization
#{\mathcal L(\bbI)=0} give #{\mathcal L(K(s))=4\ui s}. Hence
##{\mathcal L(\ue^{\ui\frac{\pi}{3}}\bbI)=\frac{4\pi\ui}{3}.}
The determinant equals #{\ue^{-2\pi\ui /3}}, whose principal
scalar logarithm is #{-2\pi\ui /3}. The determinant can reach
the scalar principal branch cut while the operator stays
strictly accretive.}

\p{For a general invertible endomorphism, a continuous
logarithm of the determinant requires a declared branch on
the domain under consideration. There is no single-valued
continuous logarithm on all invertible endomorphisms when
the dimension is at least one. In a declared basis, the loop
#{\operatorname{diag}(\ue{\ui t},1,\ldots,1)}, for
#{0\le t\le2\pi}, has determinant winding once. If
#{\det K=0}, no logarithm exists, although the
[Grassmann determinant identity](fdip-008H) still does. The
[boson-fermion cancellation](fdip-009K) uses no logarithm
branch.}
